In article <howard-AD470F.08070603012006@news.supernews.com>, Howard S Shubs <howard@shubs.net> writes:
:Are there any such adapters?

  The Q-bus does predate USB by a year or two (or more :-), obviously.

  An Integrity server, sharing the same network with the Q-bus box,
  could be pressed into service here.

  As for direct connections, I'm not aware of any -- there are pieces
  around that can provide Q-bus connectivity and there are pieces around
  that provide USB, and you might be able to build one.  Once you get
  the USB connected to the Q-bus interface (no small task), then the
  host interface requirements become interesting -- if you're not booting
  from USB, then you can boot from something (else) and connect the 
  necessary (user-written) driver(s).  If you want bootstrap support 
  from whatever processor is mastering the Q-bus, then the Q-bus side
  will have to emulate a UQSSP or similar Q-bus controller interface 
  sufficiently that the host console can use the controller.

  The Q-bus specs are available, and there are various USB prototype
  modules around.

  Not a small project, of course.



