In article <6a0vf.2171$0y2.1609@bignews2.bellsouth.net>, <blstuart@bellsouth.net> writes:
:Perhaps I should give a little background here.  (It's partly
:moot now since I've ordered a copy of Internals and Data Structures.)
:I'm writing an OS textbook and am includig a number of example
:systems as illustration of the principles I'm covering.  My
:intended use of VMS as an example is mostly in its VAX implementation.

  Yikes.  We've done a whole lot of work since OpenVMS VAX.

  64-bit addressing (upward-compatible from 32-bit addressing), the
  ports to Alpha and Itanium, etc., and the move away from various
  architectural dependencies and toward portability.  (From VAX 
  hardware, to Alpha SRM, and into OpenVMS itself on Integrity.)

:I've got a little material on things like paging and scheduling,
:but haven't really found anything that draws an architectural
:picture I can use to introduce the system in Chapter 1.

  You want the Alpha IDSM, at a minimum.

  We've added class scheduling, processor affinity, distributed
  interrupt handling, software-based interrupt services, virtual
  machine capabilities, etc.

  The VAX kernel design has not seen significant kernel changes 
  since V6.0; in well over a decade.
  
    http://h71000.www7.hp.com/openvms/os/openvms-release-history.html

  We have been quite busy in subsequent years within OpenVMS of course,
  but our statements around kernel-mode upward-compatibility on OpenVMS
  VAX (OpenVMS VAX V6.0 and later) have greatly limited our ability to
  change the VAX kernel in any significant fashion.

  Anyone looking at a text with details of OpenVMS VAX is going to be
  looking at very old information, in other words.

	--

  If you send along a proposal on what you are writing, I can pass it
  along to the appropriate folks here in OpenVMS Business Management.

