:[Please do not mail me a copy of your followup]
:
:hoff@hp.nospam spake the secret code
:<guXuf.1204$6k4.123@news.cpqcorp.net> thusly:
:
:>  As for direct connections, I'm not aware of any -- there are pieces
:>  around that can provide Q-bus connectivity and there are pieces around
:>  that provide USB, and you might be able to build one. [...]
:
:What are the Q-bus connectivity pieces?

  The Q-bus documentation, and (if you pry it off an existing module) one
  of the existing Q-bus interface chips.  You may well be able to implement
  an FPGA-based Q-bus interface, given improvements in FPGA speeds and feeds.

  Look around for the DC7041 chip or the DCK11 series, as a start, and see
  if any of these -- if you can find them -- will meet your requirements.
  Look for the DCK11-AA, DCK11-AB and the wire-wrap DCK11-AC and DCK11-AD,
  as a start.  I *think* these are the right part numbers.

  Also, the Microsystems handbook and/or the Microcomputer Interfaces
  Handbook might be of interest -- the Q-bus specs are fairly widely
  available, too, in the backs of various manuals.

  This stuff predates most of my collection of DIGITAL ephemera, so I'm
  not remotely certain that these part numbers, names or model numbers 
  are the correct ones.  Most are around twenty years old, or more, too.

  If I were going after this now -- and it was not possible to take one
  of the easier courses, such as networking with an Integrity server --
  I'd probably be looking first at an FPGA-based (Xilinx or otherwise)
  implementation.


