> Like most stuff I have, these were picked up for free.  Presumably it's 
> a matter of "less to go wrong, less to repair" and the MTBF should be 
> less than for a real disk, right?  Probably, everything else will fail 
> before they do.  (Probably when they fail, it will be the batteries, 
> right?  Can they be (easily) replaced?)  On the other hand, IF a page or 
> swap disk fails, won't that hang that process (and, if it's an important 
> process, potentially hang the entire machine)?  Although things might be 
> different in the future, at the moment the machine is 500 km away, so I 
> like to keep it operating in a "hands-off" mode, i.e. I don't want to 
> rely on someone being there to pull a disk, reboot it or whatever.
> 

When all this happens are you getting ANYTHING in errorlog?  Also, 
knowing what TYPE disk can be very helpful.  Some of those SSDs have 
ways you can poke them to find out if you're hitting some internal 
limitation or problem.

For instance, I have two EZ54s that were in lab systems that can be 
"tricked" into working well enough, but when pressed hard or if the 
power fails you have to go thru many gyrations to get them working 
again.  In a nutshell, they have a built-in battery and RZ25 long-term 
retention backup but the batteries have died and expired.  All sorts of 
weird things happen and the replacement battery packs are VERY 
expensive.  BUT if I had those, those EZ54s would be "right as rain." 
When they're working, they seem fine.  Once they encounter their 
problems, I'm back to talking to the internal processor on the drives, 
re-internally formatting, re-tricking the batteries, etc.  And, of 
course, that means the data's long gone...  A massive pain and not 
really worth the 500MB or supposed "added performance" of the SSD.

bob



