Bob Koehler wrote:
>    After being out of maintanence for a while, we had HP come in and
>    repair some problems prior to going back on maintenance.
> 
>    We had a DEC TLZ06 in a BA350 and I was using /media=compaction to
>    store my backups.  Searching for DEC TLZ06 I find references that it
>    was a 2/4GB drive accepting 120m tapes.
> 
>    The replacement drive shows up (via show dev/full) as an "ARCHIVE 
>    Python 28454-XXX", and support for compaction is not listed.  
>    Searches have found references that it's a 2.5GB drive accepting 
>    90m tapes.
> 
>    Is it true that I've lost 30m and compaction?  (IIRC TLZ04 were 60m
>    drives and could be damaged by using a 90m or 120m tape).
> 
>    Will VMS quietly ignore the /media=compaction in my backup scripts,
>    or must I change them?  (My tests seem to indicate no errors when I
>    use /media=compaction, I've no idea what the drive is really doing).
> 

First, the TLZ06 was a fairly early DAT drive.  Granted, but it SHOULD 
be capable of writing 2GB native on 90M DDS-1 cartridges and 4GB 
compressed.  If you regularly were using 120M carts, I'm surprised they 
were working correctly 100% of the time.

IF you're saying then that you had the TLZ06 replaced by HP?  If the 
drive isn't working properly and you've paid for it to be replaced and 
NOW it's under support, call 'em back.  If you had that TLZ06 replaced 
with a tapedrive of the same part number and it's not showing itself as 
a TLZ06 like it should, the drive is either an incorrect replacement or 
it's switches are not properly setup inside the SBB container.  DON'T 
open it to check them, call HP and 'splain what's up.  You replaced a 
TLZ06-VA, the replacement NEEDS to report itself as a TLZ06-VA.  If you 
can't get one that works correctly, ask your local unit manager to 
replace it with something that does work.  But at this point it would be 
imprudent to say if you've lost your data on tape or not.  The only way 
to check is with a properly working replacement.



